content.text:
Found a likely culprit before instrumenting: `Binder.bindText` (vamp.ts:160) does `el.textContent = fn(s)` unconditionally, with no equality check. Setting `textContent` always tears down the existing text node and creates a new one, even when the string is identical. Every streamed token runs `b.sync()` over the whole tree, so *every* segment span in *every* message gets its text node replaced — not just the one that's growing. A live selection whose endpoints live in those nodes has its anchor/focus nodes ripped out from under it, and with the mouse still down the browser re-extends from whatever it can salvage. That matches "flickers and behaves unpredictably", and matches your observation that nothing is remounting: it's below the remount level, at the text node.

Same shape in `bindClass` (`el.className = ...` every sync) — harmless for selection, but it does force style recalc per token.

To confirm it rather than take my word, paste this in the console with a selection held (it logs which text nodes get replaced and whether the selection endpoints were among them):

```js
const t = document.querySelector('[data-ref] ul') ?? document.querySelector('ul');
const sel = window.getSelection();
let anchor = sel.anchorNode, focus = sel.focusNode;
new MutationObserver((records) => {
  const removed = records.flatMap(r => [...r.removedNodes]);
  if (!removed.length) return;
  console.log('removed text nodes:', removed.length,
    'anchor killed:', removed.includes(anchor),
    'focus killed:', removed.includes(focus),
    'sel now:', sel.toString().slice(0, 40));
  anchor = sel.anchorNode; focus = sel.focusNode;
}).observe(t, { childList: true, subtree: true, characterData: true });
```

If that confirms it, the fix is two lines — guard the write:

```ts
const next = fn(s);
if (el.textContent !== next) el.textContent = next;
```

That kills the flicker for every message except the one actively streaming. For that one you'd need to append to the existing text node instead of replacing it (`node.appendData(next.slice(node.length))` when `next.startsWith(node.data)`), which preserves offsets and so preserves the selection. Want me to make either change?

